 There is a wonderful symmetry between this result and #e-inverse-1. In fact, what we have just shown is that 
\begin_inset Formula $c(k)$
\end_inset

 is the inverse Fourier transform of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi(x,0)$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi(x,0)$
\end_inset

 is the Fourier transform of 
\begin_inset Formula $c(k)$
\end_inset

, which you learnt about in Mathematical Physics 202. Importantly, this shows that for a free particle, we now have the general solution for an arbitrary initial condition 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi(x,0)$
\end_inset

. It is worth pausing to take in the implications of this. In principle, you now have the ability to specify an arbitrary initial wavefunction for the particle 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi(x,t_0)$
\end_inset

 (which of course must be normalised), and from this determine the wavefunction of the particle at all other times, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi(x,t)$
\end_inset

. Combining #e-c-from-psi with #e-wavepacket (and taking care to introduce a number dummy variable 
\begin_inset Formula $x'$
\end_inset

 for the integration), we arrive at the â rather formidable looking â general solution 
